.TH DVBTX-SOURCES 7 2026-11-08 "dvbtx 0.3.0" "dvbtx manual"
.SH NAME
dvbtx-sources \- source syntax and behaviour of the dvbtx services
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
[[carrier.service]]
name = "Info"
source = "SOURCE"                          # or ["SOURCE", "SOURCE", ...]
backup = "/var/lib/dvbtx/backup.ts"        # or false
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each service (channel) of a
.B dvbtx
multiplex plays a
.IR source :
a file, a folder, a stream URL, a capture device, a pipe or a command.
The
.B source
key of a
.B [[carrier.service]]
table (see
.BR dvbtx.toml (5)),
the
.B \-\-service
option of
.BR dvbtx (1),
.BR dvbtx\-channel (1)
and the web page all take the same strings.
The
.B backup
key takes the same strings too.
.PP
Every source runs in a process of its own, usually
.BR ffmpeg (1),
sometimes fed by a producer
.RB ( streamlink (1),
.BR yt\-dlp ,
a command), that turns it into a single\-program MPEG\-TS sent over UDP to
.BR 127.0.0.1 :\fIport\fR
(port
.BR ingest.base_port " + the input number)."
The engine reads it, re\-times every packet, restamps the PCRs and remaps the PIDs into the
multiplex.
A source that crashes, freezes or lags never disturbs the others: its service switches to its
backup, and the process is restarted.
Only
.B udp+direct://
sources skip ffmpeg and are received by the engine itself.
.SH SOURCE FORMS
.SS Files
.TP
.IR /path/file.mkv ", " file:///path/file.mkv
A local file, looped
.RB ( "loop = true" ,
ffmpeg
.BR "\-stream_loop \-1" ).
A path that is a FIFO is read as a stream instead.
.TP
.IR dir:/path/folder ", " /path/folder
Every video file of the folder
.RI ( .mkv ,
.IR .mp4 ,
.IR .ts ,
.IR .m2ts ,
.IR .mov ,
.IR .avi ,
.IR .webm ,
.IR .mpg ,
.IR .mpeg ,
.IR .flv ,
.IR .m4v ,
.IR .wmv ),
in name order, as a playlist: one ffmpeg per file, the next one started as soon as a file ends,
back to the first after the last.
Files of different formats may be mixed; the engine handles the change of timeline and PMT.
.SS HTTP and HLS
.TP
.IR http://... ", " https://...
A progressive stream or an HLS playlist, with reconnection on network errors (15 s read timeout).
An HLS URL (one containing
.BR m3u8 )
is read through streamlink or ffmpeg according to
.B hls
(see
.BR "HLS READER" ).
.TP
.IB URL #variant= N
Only variant
.I N
of an HLS master playlist (0 = the first, as
.B ffprobe \-show_programs
numbers them); ffmpeg otherwise takes the first variant listed.
Read by ffmpeg.
.TP
.IB URL #quality= Q
The quality streamlink names
.I Q
.RB ( 720p ,
.BR 3300k ,
.BR best ,
.BR 1000k,worst ...;
default
.BR best ),
for an HLS URL; read through streamlink whatever
.B hls
says (an error if it is not installed).
.PP
The fragment applies to that source only, so that a list can fall back to a lower quality of the
same stream, and is never sent to the server.
.TP
.BI m3u: PATH_OR_URL # ENTRY
An entry of an M3U playlist, local or fetched over HTTP: matched by its title,
.B tvg\-name
or
.B tvg\-id
(case, spaces and punctuation ignored), or by its index from 0 when
.I ENTRY
is a number; without
.BI # ENTRY ,
the first entry.
Resolved again at each start of the source.
.TP
.BI streamlink: URL \fR[\fB#quality= Q \fR]
Anything streamlink has a plugin for (Dailymotion, YouTube, HLS...), written by streamlink on a
pipe to ffmpeg; quality
.B best
by default.
.TP
.BI twitch:// CHANNEL \fR[\fB?quality= Q \fR]
A Twitch channel, through streamlink with its ads disabled; without streamlink, the
.B best
format given by yt\-dlp.
.TP
.BI ytdlp: URL
Anything yt\-dlp can read (YouTube lives...), with the format
.B ytdlp_format
(default: H.264 up to 1080p with AAC).
The stream URLs are resolved again at each start (they expire).
When yt\-dlp gives separate video and audio streams, both are read with their own timestamps
to stay in sync.
.TP
.BI htsp:// USER : PASS @ HOST :9982/ CHANNEL\fR,\ \fBtvh://\fR...
A tvheadend channel, by name or number, read from its HTTP streaming port (the HTSP port
minus one) with the
.B pass
profile
.RB ( ?profile=...
chooses another).
.SS Network streams
.TP
.BI rtmp:// HOST / APP / KEY\fR,\ \fBrtmps://\fR...
RTMP pull.
.TP
.BI rtmp://0.0.0.0:1935/ APP / KEY ?listen
RTMP received: dvbtx listens, and OBS (or any RTMP client) streams to
.BI rtmp:// this\-host / APP / KEY\fR.
No timeout: the service plays its backup until a client connects.
.TP
.BI rtsp:// ...
RTSP over TCP
.RB ( rtsp_transport
changes it), 15 s timeout.
.TP
.BI srt:// ...\fR,\ \fBudp://\fR...,\ \fBrtp://\fR...,\ \fBtcp://\fR...
Read by ffmpeg with its own options in the URL
.RB ( srt://0.0.0.0:9000?mode=listener ).
.B udp://
gets a large FIFO, overruns tolerated, 15 s timeout.
.TP
.BI udp+direct:// HOST : PORT\fR,\ \fBdirect://\fR...
An MPEG\-TS on UDP, unicast or multicast, received by the engine itself: no process, no
re\-encoding, no codec check.
It must carry DVB codecs.
.B program
selects a program of a multi\-program stream (0: the first).
A plain
.B udp://
source with
.B direct = true
is the same.
.SS Capture and generated sources
These are always encoded (see
.BR "COPY OR ENCODE" ).
.TP
.BI v4l2: DEVICE ? OPTIONS
A webcam or HDMI capture card
.RI ( /dev/video0
when
.I DEVICE
is empty).
Options, joined with
.BR & :
.BI size= WxH\fR,
.BI fps= N\fR,
.BI format= F
(input format such as
.BR mjpeg ),
.BI audio= A\fR.
.TP
.BI screen: DISPLAY ? OPTIONS\fR,\ \fBx11:\fR...
The X11 screen
.RB ( screen::0.0 ;
.B $DISPLAY
when empty):
.BI size= WxH\fR,
.BI fps= N
(default 25),
.BI audio= A\fR.
.TP
.BI lavfi: GRAPH\fR[\fB?audio=\fIA\fR], \fBtestsrc:\fR...
An ffmpeg lavfi source graph, read at real time
.RB ( lavfi:testsrc2=size=1280x720:rate=25 ,
the default;
.BR lavfi:smptehdbars=size=1920x1080:rate=25 ).
.PP
.I A
is
.B silence
(default of
.B v4l2:
and
.BR screen: ),
.B sine
(1 kHz, default of
.BR lavfi: ),
.BR none ,
.BI alsa: DEV\fR,
.BI pulse: DEV\fR,
.BI pipewire: DEV
(through PulseAudio),
.BI jack: DEV
.RB ( default
when
.I DEV
is empty), or any other ffmpeg input.
.SS Pipes and commands
.TP
.BI pipe: PATH
A named pipe, in any container ffmpeg understands
.RB ( "mkfifo /run/dvbtx/in.fifo" ,
then
.BR "any_program > /run/dvbtx/in.fifo" ).
.TP
.BR stdin: ", " stdin ", " \-
The standard input of dvbtx
.RB ( "some_program | dvbtx run \-c ..." ).
.TP
.BI exec: COMMAND
The standard output of a shell command
.RB ( "exec:streamlink \-\-stdout https://www.twitch.tv/x best" ),
restarted with ffmpeg.
.PP
.BR exec: ,
.BR pipe: ,
.B stdin:
sources, and the
.B ffmpeg_input
/
.B ffmpeg_output
options, are refused from the web API and from
.B dvbtx channel
talking to it, unless
.BR "web.allow_exec_sources = true" ;
the configuration file may always use them.
.SH HLS READER
ffmpeg's HLS reader stalls at the discontinuities of a live playlist (an ad, the next episode)
and falls behind short playlists; streamlink hands the segments on as they are, and ffmpeg's
MPEG\-TS reader rebases the timestamps there.
The
.B hls
setting (per service or in
.BR [defaults] )
chooses:
.TP
.B auto
(default) through streamlink when it is installed and the service asks nothing that only ffmpeg
does: no
.BI # variant ,
no
.B ffmpeg_input
option, no
.B map
on programs
.RB ( 0:p:... ).
.TP
.B ffmpeg
always ffmpeg (but
.B #quality=
still forces streamlink).
.TP
.B streamlink
always streamlink; an error if it is not installed.
.SH COPY OR ENCODE
Sources are copied
.RB ( "\-c copy" ,
the streams selected by
.BR map :
the first video and every audio track by default), except:
.IP \(bu 2
capture sources
.RB ( v4l2: ,
.BR screen: ,
.BR lavfi: ),
always encoded;
.IP \(bu 2
.B encode = true
or an
.B encode = { ... }
table: encoded with the
.B [defaults.encode]
settings, changed by the table;
.IP \(bu 2
without an explicit
.BR encode ,
and with
.B codec_check
(the default), files, folders, HTTP URLs, M3U entries and yt\-dlp streams are probed first with
ffprobe: video other than H.264, HEVC or MPEG\-2/1 (VP9, AV1...) is encoded; audio not in
.B copy_audio_codecs
(Opus...) is encoded alone, the video copied.
.PP
.B encode = false
forces the copy without probing.
A re\-encoded service with
.B video_bitrate = \(dqauto\(dq
takes its share of what the multiplex has left (see
.B bitrate_weight
and
.B max_bitrate
in
.BR dvbtx.toml (5)).
.SS GPU transcoding
When the codec is a generic one
.RB ( libx264 ,
.BR libx265 ,
.BR mpeg2video )
and
.B hwaccel
is
.B \(dq\(dq
or
.BR auto ,
the encode goes to a GPU when one works, else to the CPU:
.IP \(bu 2
the display adapters (Intel, AMD, NVIDIA) are detected at every start; those allowed by
.B ingest.gpu
are used;
.IP \(bu 2
every way to use them (VAAPI with each VA driver, low power or not, QSV, NVENC; the whole chain
on the GPU, decoding on it with frames copied to memory, or only the encoding) is tested once
per codec family on a 1080p to 720p capped\-rate transcode, kept if the output has the right
codec and size and stays under its bit rate cap; among the ways at least half as fast as the
fastest, the one using the least CPU is preferred; the learning runs
again, in the background with the CPU meanwhile, only when the adapters, their drivers or ffmpeg
change
.RI ( /var/lib/dvbtx/gpu.json ;
.B dvbtx gpu
shows it,
.B dvbtx gpu learn
redoes it);
.IP \(bu 2
each new transcode goes to the adapter with the most speed left;
a way that makes ffmpeg fail twice within 15 s of its start is not used again for that source,
which moves to the next way, then to the CPU.
.PP
.BR "hwaccel = \(dqvaapi\(dq" ,
.B \(dqqsv\(dq
or
.B \(dqnvenc\(dq
restricts the choice to that kind;
.B \(dqcpu\(dq
never uses a GPU.
An explicit GPU codec
.RB ( h264_vaapi ,
.BR h264_nvenc ...)
is used as given, without the learning.
.SH READING PACE
.B readrate = \(dqauto\(dq
reads at real time (ffmpeg
.BR \-re )
every source that does not arrive at real time by itself: files, folders, HTTP and HLS, M3U,
yt\-dlp, streamlink, Twitch, tvheadend, commands.
Live inputs (RTMP, RTSP, SRT, UDP, RTP, TCP, V4L2, screen, pipes, FIFOs, stdin) are read as
they come.
Network inputs read with
.B \-re
may catch up at
.B readrate_catchup
times real time (2) after a slow segment.
A source arriving faster than real time raises an alert.
.SH SEVERAL SOURCES PER CHANNEL
.B source
may be a list, in order of preference:
.PP
.RS
.EX
source = ["http://provider/u/p/289", "http://provider/u/p/319", "http://other/info.m3u8"]
.EE
.RE
.PP
The first one plays.
The supervisor leaves the source playing, stops it, and starts the next one (after the last, the
first again), the channel playing its backup meanwhile, when:
.IP \(bu 2
it has delivered nothing for
.B failover_s
(10 s), or has not started within
.B source_start_s
(30 s);
.IP \(bu 2
it is unstable: the channel fell back to its backup
.B unstable_switches
times (3) within
.B unstable_window_s
(600 s; 0 switches: never), or stayed on it
.BR failover_s " + " return_ms
(20 s).
.PP
A source is stopped before the next one starts, so an IPTV account allowing one connection can
be given several of its streams.
.PP
While not on the first source, the preferred ones are tried again in order every
.B preferred_retry_s
(300 s; 0: never), beside the one playing: a candidate that delivers for 2 s takes over without
a cut; one that does not deliver within
.B source_start_s
is abandoned.
A source that was left is not tried again before a delay that doubles each time it is left
(from
.B preferred_retry_s
up to an hour); that delay is reset once the source has played 30 min.
.PP
When no source works, the backup plays.
The web page,
.B dvbtx channel list
and the alerts tell which source is on air.
.SH CACHE
.B buffer_ms
is the delay between the arrival of a source and its transmission.
Larger, an irregular source goes through without lost packets nor switch to the backup, the
channel being later than live.
.B auto
(default) is 6000 ms for live sources delivered in segments, which arrive in bursts at the live
edge
.RB ( twitch:// ,
.BR ytdlp: ,
.BR streamlink: ,
HTTP URLs containing
.BR m3u8 ,
youtube.com, youtu.be and twitch.tv URLs), 1000 ms otherwise.
Per service: 100 to 30000.
A source is taken as down only once its cache has played out.
Changing the cache restarts the source beside the old one, without a cut.
.SH BACKUP CLIP
.B backup
is played while the service's sources deliver nothing: after
.B switch_timeout_ms
(3000) of silence, once the cache has played out, the engine switches the service to it on a
key frame; it switches back once the source has been stable for
.B return_ms
(10000).
.PP
The backup is any source form, looped, copied unless
.BR "backup_encode = true" ;
one process serves every service that names the same backup.
The default is none; the packaged configuration sets
.IR /var/lib/dvbtx/backup.ts ,
made at installation by
.BR "dvbtx make\-backup" :
a 10 s 1080p H.264 clip with a text
.RB ( \-\-text )
or a picture
.RB ( \-\-image ),
silence or a 1 kHz tone
.RB ( \-\-tone ),
.B \-\-duration
seconds, written to
.B \-o
.RI ( /var/lib/dvbtx/backup.ts ).
.B backup = false
on a service plays nothing instead.
.SH SUPERVISION
.TP
.B Restart
A source process that exits is restarted after
.B ingest.restart_min_s
(1 s), doubled at each failure up to
.B ingest.restart_max_s
(30 s), back to the minimum after 60 s of running.
A producer (streamlink, a command) that dies takes its ffmpeg with it, and both restart.
.TP
.B Stall
A source process running but delivering nothing for
.B ingest.stall_restart_s
(20 s: a frozen playlist, a half\-open connection) is restarted, so that the source comes back
as soon as it is available.
.TP
.B Timestamp discontinuities
A source whose ffmpeg reports
.B ingest.discontinuity_restart
(10) timestamp discontinuities within 30 s does not recover by itself: it is started again
beside the running one and swapped in without a cut (a source received on a local port,
.BR udp:// ,
.BR rtp:// ,
.BR srt:// ,
.BR ?listen ,
is stopped first: a short cut).
.TP
.B New PIDs
When ffmpeg reports a video stream appearing mid\-way (an ad inserted on PIDs of its own), the
streams mapped at the start fall silent: ffmpeg is started again at once (not twice within 10 s).
.TP
.B HLS standby
When streamlink reports a discontinuity of an HLS stream (an ad, the next episode), its output
may stop there for minutes.
A second copy of the source is started beside it from the live edge, already past the
discontinuity, and kept ready: it takes over without a cut as soon as the source in use stalls
for 2 s or moves its video to new PIDs.
It is stopped after 60 s if nothing stalled, or after 25 s if the source stalled and the standby
is not delivering yet; the choice among several sources waits while a standby is starting.
.TP
.B Paused sources
The sources of a multiplex stopped from the web page
.RB ( "enabled = false" ),
or of a multiplex whose radio is missing or unplugged, are stopped (paused) and the choice among
several sources is suspended; they start again when the multiplex is started or its radio is
back.
Backup clips keep running.
.SH CHANGES WHILE RUNNING
.BR dvbtx\-channel (1),
the web page, and
.B systemctl reload dvbtx
after an edit of the file change the source of one channel without touching the others: the new
source starts beside the old one, which plays until the new one has a video key frame due, and
the switch is made there.
A change of a source setting
.RB ( encode ,
.BR ytdlp_format ,
.BR readrate ,
.BR map ,
.BR buffer_ms ...)\&
restarts the source the same way.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/dvbtx/dvbtx.toml
The configuration.
.TP
.I /var/lib/dvbtx/backup.ts
Backup clip of the packaged configuration.
.TP
.I /var/lib/dvbtx/gpu.json
Learnt GPU results
.RB ( ingest.gpu_cache ).
.TP
.I /usr/share/dvbtx/configs/sources.toml
One service of each source form.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
[[carrier.service]]
name = "Film"
source = "/srv/videos/film.mkv"

[[carrier.service]]
name = "Playlist"
source = "dir:/srv/videos/playlist"
loop = true

[[carrier.service]]
name = "France 24"
source = "m3u:/srv/iptv/france.m3u#France 24"

[[carrier.service]]
name = "News"                     # FHD, then SD of the same stream, then another provider
source = ["https://host/live.m3u8#variant=3",
          "https://host/live.m3u8#variant=0",
          "http://other.example/news.m3u8#quality=720p"]
preferred_retry_s = 600

[[carrier.service]]
name = "YouTube"
source = "ytdlp:https://www.youtube.com/@SkyNews/live"
ytdlp_format = "bv*[vcodec^=avc1][height<=720]+ba/b[height<=720]"
buffer_ms = 8000

[[carrier.service]]
name = "Twitch"
source = "twitch://some_channel?quality=720p"

[[carrier.service]]
name = "Tvheadend"
source = "htsp://user:pass@tvh.lan:9982/France 2"

[[carrier.service]]
name = "OBS"
source = "rtmp://0.0.0.0:1935/live/obs?listen"

[[carrier.service]]
name = "Camera"
source = "rtsp://cam.lan:554/stream1"

[[carrier.service]]
name = "Multicast"
source = "udp+direct://239.0.0.1:2000"

[[carrier.service]]
name = "HDMI"
source = "v4l2:/dev/video2?size=1920x1080&fps=25&audio=alsa:hw:2,0"
encode = { video_bitrate = "6M", deinterlace = true }

[[carrier.service]]
name = "Screen"
source = "screen::0.0?size=1920x1080&fps=25&audio=pulse:default"
encode = { hwaccel = "cpu", size = "1280x720" }

[[carrier.service]]
name = "Pattern"
source = "lavfi:smptehdbars=size=1920x1080:rate=25"
backup = false

[[carrier.service]]
name = "Command"
source = "exec:streamlink \-\-stdout https://www.twitch.tv/some_channel best"
.EE
.PP
On the command line:
.PP
.EX
sudo dvbtx \-\-driver hackrf \-\-channel 22 \e
     \-\-service \(dqPattern=lavfi:testsrc2=size=1280x720:rate=25\(dq \e
     \-\-service \(dqMovie=/srv/videos/movie.mkv,lcn=2\(dq
dvbtx channel source 3 http://a.example/fhd.ts http://a.example/hd.ts
dvbtx channel source 5 \(dqytdlp:https://www.youtube.com/@SkyNews/live\(dq \-\-cache 8000
dvbtx make\-backup \-\-text \(dqBack in a moment\(dq \-o /var/lib/dvbtx/backup.ts
.EE
